.SH NAME
md_FAQ \- ❓SystemEx FAQ 
.PP

.SH "Why does SystemEx contain duplicated structures?"
.PP
To keep subsystems isolated and easier to debug\&. 
.br
 For example, \fRMap<TKey, TValue>\fP uses \fRPair<TKey, TValue>[]\fP instead of \fRVector<Pair<\&.\&.\&.>>\fP to avoid deep cross‑dependencies\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why are there so many numeric types?"
.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP is designed for deterministic engine development\&. 
.br
 Types like \fRHalf16\fP, \fRHalf16b\fP, \fRRatio\fP, \fRFast_Int\fP, and vector/matrix families provide explicit numeric semantics\&.

.PP
.PP
.SS "Why does SystemEx use \fR#if USE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE\fP blocks?"
\fBSystemEx\fP contains optional development blocks guarded by \fR#if USE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE\fP\&. These sections include experimental features, work‑in‑progress implementations, debugging utilities, or partially completed subsystems\&. They are intentionally excluded from normal builds to keep the repository clean, stable, and free from unfinished code paths\&.

.PP
Developers who want to test or contribute to experimental areas may enable these blocks by defining \fRUSE_DEVBUILD_UNSTABLE\fP in their build configuration\&. This approach prevents unstable or incomplete features from affecting the main code base, avoids accidental API exposure, and ensures that ongoing work does not interfere with stable modules or public releases\&.

.PP
.PP
.SS "Why is BitView dangerous when used incorrectly?"
BitView exposes a live bit-level view over a referenced integer\&. Because the span mutates the underlying value directly, using it on control variables or in Ring mode can lead to severe program instability\&.

.PP
\fBDangerous patterns include:\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Creating a BitView over a loop counter (\fRref i\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Creating a BitView over variables that control program flow
.IP "\(bu" 2
Using Ring mode inside \fRforeach\fP without a manual break condition
.PP

.PP
These patterns can corrupt indices, cause infinite loops, or produce undefined execution paths\&. BitView should only be used on dedicated data variables that are intentionally manipulated at the bit level\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include LightThread?"
.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP provides \fRLightThread\fP as a deterministic, minimal thread model built specifically for engine loops, schedulers, and low‑level systems\&. Unlike the standard \&.NET \fRThread\fP or \fRTask\fP abstractions, \fRLightThread\fP exposes a predictable wait–signal lifecycle using only two primitives:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
a user‑space lock (\fRLightLock\fP) controlling the logical wait state 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
an OS‑level \fRAutoResetEvent\fP controlling the physical block state 
.br

.PP

.PP
A \fRLightThread\fP always starts in a locked wait state:

.PP
.RS 4
"m_waitState = new LightLock(); 
.br
 m_waitState\&.Lock();" 
.br
 
.RE
.PP

.PP
and only continues when explicitly awakened through \fRSignal()\fP\&. Waiting is performed via \fRLightConditionVariable\fP, which temporarily releases a lock, enqueues the thread in a wait queue, and blocks until a wake event occurs\&.

.PP
This design avoids scheduler‑driven behavior, hidden state machines, and thread‑pool semantics\&. It provides deterministic pause/resume behavior, explicit cooperative suspension, predictable wake‑up ordering, and minimal overhead\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include ThreadEx?"
.PP
\fRThreadEx\fP is a managed thread wrapper that provides deterministic lifecycle control using an \fREventGroup<Fast_Int>\fP for signaling and \fRLightLock\fP for synchronization\&. Instead of relying on \&.NET’s monitor or task scheduler, \fRThreadEx\fP models thread state transitions explicitly:

.PP
.RS 4
"All state transitions are represented as bit flags inside an EventGroup<Fast_Int>\&." 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Reserved bits include:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB0\fP — Joinable 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB1\fP — Started 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB3\fP — Pause requested 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB4\fP — Continue requested 
.br

.PP

.PP
This allows the thread to cooperatively pause, resume, abort, or kill itself based on explicit bit‑flags rather than runtime heuristics\&. The main loop checks these bits directly:

.PP
.RS 4
"if (IsSuspend()) { \&.\&.\&. } else { OnTask\&.Invoke(\&.\&.\&.); }" 
.RE
.PP

.PP
The result is a predictable, engine‑oriented thread model suitable for simulation loops, schedulers, and low‑level systems\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include EventGroup?"
.PP
\fREventGroup<TFastType>\fP is a lightweight bit‑flag signaling system inspired by FreeRTOS event groups\&. It uses a fast bit‑storage type (\fRFast_Int\fP) and a \fRLightLock\fP for synchronization\&. Each bit represents an event, and threads can wait on specific bits:

.PP
.RS 4
"Waits until the bit at the specified position becomes set… using Thread\&.Yield\&." 
.RE
.PP

.PP
EventGroup provides:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
deterministic event signaling 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
no kernel‑level wait handles 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
no monitor/condition variable overhead 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
predictable busy‑wait semantics 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
extremely low latency 
.br

.PP

.PP
It is ideal for thread coordination, state machines, and engine subsystems\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include LightConditionVariable?"
.PP
\fRLightConditionVariable\fP provides a minimal wait queue for threads\&. Instead of using \&.NET’s \fRMonitor\&.Wait\fP or \fRAutoResetEvent\fP directly, it implements a cooperative wait mechanism:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
the waiting thread releases a lock 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
it is added to a wait queue 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
it blocks on its own \fRAutoResetEvent\fP 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
it reacquires the lock after waking 
.br

.PP

.PP
This matches the behavior of condition variables in POSIX and FreeRTOS, but implemented in pure C# with deterministic semantics\&.

.PP
It is designed for:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
engine loops 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
schedulers 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
lock‑free or low‑lock systems 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
predictable wake‑up ordering 
.br

.PP

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include LightLock?"
.PP
\fRLightLock\fP is a minimal user‑space lock used throughout the threading subsystem\&. It avoids OS‑level mutexes and provides:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
extremely low overhead 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
predictable behavior 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
no kernel transitions 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
no recursion 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
no hidden scheduling effects 
.br

.PP

.PP
It is ideal for small critical sections and deterministic engine code\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include LightCountingSpinlock?"
.PP
\fRLightCountingSpinlock\fP is a pure user‑space atomic spinlock designed for high‑frequency locking scenarios where kernel mutexes are too slow\&. It is intended for:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
short critical sections 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
high‑performance numeric code 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
engine subsystems 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
lock‑free‑adjacent algorithms 
.br

.PP

.PP
It avoids blocking entirely and relies on atomic operations\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include EventGroup‑based LightTask?"
.PP
\fRLightTask\fP is a lightweight task abstraction built on \fREventGroup\fP and \fRLightThread\fP\&. It provides:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
cooperative pause/resume 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
event‑driven state transitions 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
deterministic task lifecycle 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
predictable scheduling behavior 
.br

.PP

.PP
It is ideal for:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
engine task graphs 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
simulation steps 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
worker pipelines 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
custom schedulers 
.br

.PP

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx avoid \&.NET Task and ThreadPool?"
.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP intentionally avoids:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
thread‑pool scheduling 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
async/await state machines 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
continuation chains 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
hidden runtime heuristics 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
unpredictable wake‑up timing 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
automatic load balancing 
.br

.PP

.PP
These features are powerful but unsuitable for deterministic engine code\&.

.PP
\fBSystemEx\fP threading is designed for:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
explicit control 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
predictable timing 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
deterministic state transitions 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
minimal overhead 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
debugging clarity 
.br

.PP

.PP
This makes it ideal for simulation engines, rendering pipelines, numeric processing, and custom schedulers\&.

.PP
.PP
.SS "Where can I find a list of predefined colors?"
All predefined colors in \fBSystemEx\fP are located in the namespace \fR\fBSystemEx\&.Drawing\fP\fP\&. Instead of storing all colors in a single class, \fBSystemEx\fP organizes them into logical color groups\&. Each group contains only colors of a specific hue family, such as red, blue, green, brown, purple, etc\&.

.PP
This structure makes it easy to find colors by category\&. For example, if you are looking for a red tone, you only need to browse the \fRRedColors\fP class\&. If you need a blue tone, you check \fRBlueColors\fP, and so on\&.

.PP
Available color groups include:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRBrownColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRBlueColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRPinkColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRPurpelColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRRedColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fROrangeColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRYellowColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGreenColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRCyanColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRWhiteColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGreyColors\fP
.PP

.PP
Each group provides a curated selection of common web colors as \fRColorR8G8B8\fP instances\&. This avoids searching through hundreds of unrelated colors and keeps the API clean, predictable, and easy to navigate\&.

.PP
.PP
.SS "Why are the colors split into multiple classes instead of one big list?"
\fBSystemEx\fP groups colors by hue family to make them easier to find and use\&. Instead of searching through a single class with dozens of unrelated colors, you can directly open the group that matches the color you need\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Need a red tone? → \fRRedColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Need a blue tone? → \fRBlueColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Need a warm brown tone? → \fRBrownColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Need something pastel? → \fRPinkColors\fP or \fRPurpelColors\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Need grayscale? → \fRGreyColors\fP
.PP

.PP
This organization is more intuitive, reduces search time, and keeps the API clean and logically structured\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why does SystemEx include an interop subsystem?"
.PP
To support dynamic loading of native libraries across Linux, macOS, and Windows\&.

.PP
.PP
.SH "Why is the random subsystem custom?"
.PP
ISAAC provide deterministic, high‑quality random generation suitable for simulation and procedural generation\&.

.PP
.PP
